From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 15:48:43 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 176/181] input: misc: Egis ET5xx fingerprint sensor control
 for a secure SPI bus

On the Samsung Galaxy Tab S3 the Egis ET510 fingerprint sensor's SPI bus
(BLSP1 QUP6, TLMM 25-28) belongs to TrustZone: the sensor is driven by a
QSEE app, and Linux only switches its power (BTP_LDO_EN) and reset
(BTP_RST_N), runs the QUP's clocks for the secure world at the rate the
HAL asks for, and delivers the data-ready interrupt. This driver does that
through the ABI of Samsung's et5xx-spi driver, which the vendor biometric
library (libbauthserver) uses: /dev/esfp0 with EGIS_IOC_MESSAGE and the
control opcodes (power, reset, SPI clock on/off, sensor type, DRDY
interrupt init/close/abort), poll() for a finger, and the
/sys/class/fingerprint/fingerprint attributes (name, vendor, adm,
type_check, bfs_values, intcnt, resetcnt). The register, image and NVM
opcodes are refused: they are the secure world's.

Binding: compatible "egistec,et5xx-ctrl", reset-gpios, ldo-enable-gpios,
drdy-gpios, clocks "core" (the QUP SPI clock) and "iface" (AHB), optional
vdd-supply, egistec,chipid. On an SM-T820 (dts/test/test-fp-ctrl.dts:
PM8994 MPP 2/8, TLMM 131, GCC_BLSP1_QUP6_SPI_APPS_CLK/AHB): the power,
clock (12.5 MHz asked, 15 MHz set) and reset sequence runs with no
reaction from TrustZone.

Signed-off-by: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit ac97a754fa6d9d5655fdd6b77b2cd26b5b759c96)
